  ($q$geo-644$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 1, null, $q$In a triangle, two of the angles measure 34° and 53°. What is the measure of the third angle, in degrees?$q$, $q$["93","103","273","56"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angles of a triangle sum to 180°: 180° − 34° − 53° = 93°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-645$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$A right triangle has legs of length 8 and 15. What is the length of its hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["15","18","20","17"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$√(8² + 15²) = √289 = 17.$q$),
  ($q$geo-646$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines ℓ and m are parallel and are cut by a transversal. One angle measures (4x − 2)° and its alternate interior angle measures (x + 9)°. What is the value of x?$q$, $q$["169","11","13","35"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Alternate interior angles are equal: 4x − 2 = x + 9 → 3x = 11 → x = 11.$q$),
  ($q$geo-647$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 2, null, $q$In a triangle, the measures of the angles are x°, (3x + 5)°, and 103°. What is the value of x?$q$, $q$["21","18","52","26"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$x + (3x + 5) + 103 = 180 → 4x = 72 → x = 18.$q$),
  ($q$geo-648$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 2, null, $q$In a triangle, the measures of the angles are x°, (3x + 7)°, and 85°. What is the value of x?$q$, $q$["43","19","25","22"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$x + (3x + 7) + 85 = 180 → 4x = 88 → x = 22.$q$),
  ($q$geo-649$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 2, null, $q$The total surface area of a cube is 726 square units. What is the volume of the cube, in cubic units?$q$, $q$["66","7986","121","1331"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Each of the 6 faces has area 121, so the edge length is 11. Volume = 11³ = 1331.$q$),
  ($q$geo-650$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 1, null, $q$A rectangular box has length 8, width 11, and height 6. What is the volume of the box?$q$, $q$["616","528","404","25"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$V = lwh = 8 × 11 × 6 = 528.$q$),
  ($q$geo-651$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 2, null, $q$In a 30°-60°-90° triangle, the shorter leg has length 7. What is the length of the longer leg?$q$, $q$["7√3","14","7√2","21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The longer leg is the shorter leg times √3: 7√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-652$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 2, null, $q$The midpoint of segment AB is (4, 8). If A has coordinates (−1, 5), what are the coordinates of B?$q$, $q$["(3, 13)","(5, 3)","(10, 10)","(9, 11)"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$B = (2·4 − (−1), 2·8 − 5) = (9, 11).$q$),
  ($q$geo-653$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$An equilateral triangle has side length 2. What is its area?$q$, $q$["4√3","1√3","1","2√3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The height is (2/2)√3 = 1√3. The area is (1/2)(2)(1√3) = 1√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-654$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation x² + y² − 2x + 12y = 27 defines a circle in the xy-plane. What is the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["27","8","2","9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Complete the square: (x − 1)² + (y + 6)² = 27 + 1 + 36 = 64. The center is (1, −6) and the radius is 8.$q$),
  ($q$geo-655$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 2, null, $q$In a right triangle, one acute angle measures 15°. If sin 15° = p, which of the following is equal to cos 75°?$q$, $q$["1 − p","1/p","√(1 − p²)","p"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Sine and cosine of complementary angles are equal: cos(90° − x°) = sin x°. So cos 75° = sin 15° = p.$q$),
  ($q$geo-656$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 1, null, $q$An equilateral triangle has side length 6. What is its area?$q$, $q$["9","18√3","36√3","9√3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The height is (6/2)√3 = 3√3. The area is (1/2)(6)(3√3) = 9√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-657$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 1, null, $q$In a triangle, two of the angles measure 68° and 78°. What is the measure of the third angle, in degrees?$q$, $q$["112","34","44","22"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Angles of a triangle sum to 180°: 180° − 68° − 78° = 34°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-658$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 2, null, $q$The midpoint of segment AB is (1, −3). If A has coordinates (−6, −3), what are the coordinates of B?$q$, $q$["(7, 0)","(9, −4)","(−5, −6)","(8, −3)"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$B = (2·1 − (−6), 2·−3 − (−3)) = (8, −3).$q$),
  ($q$geo-659$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 2, null, $q$For an acute angle θ, sin θ = 12/13. What is the value of tan θ?$q$, $q$["13/5","5/12","12/13","12/5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Draw a right triangle with opposite side 12 and hypotenuse 13; the adjacent side is √(13² − 12²) = 5. So tan θ = 12/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-660$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, null, $q$A circle has an area of 64π square units. What is the circumference of the circle?$q$, $q$["64π","16π","32π","128π"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$πr² = 64π gives r = 8, so the circumference is 2πr = 16π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-661$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, null, $q$A sector of a circle has radius 12 and a central angle of 30°. What is the area of the sector?$q$, $q$["24π","6π","144π","12π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Area = (30/360)(π · 12²) = 12π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-662$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation x² + y² + 2x − 2y = 34 defines a circle in the xy-plane. What is the x-coordinate of the center of the circle?$q$, $q$["−1","1","2","0"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Complete the square: (x + 1)² + (y − 1)² = 34 + 1 + 1 = 36. The center is (−1, 1) and the radius is 6.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 10x + y² + 4y + 4 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the length of the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["2","25","√29","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² − 10x + 25) + (y² + 4y + 4) = −4 + 25 + 4, so (x − 5)² + (y + 2)² = 25. The radius is √25 = 5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 82 centimeters. A chord of the circle is 18 centimeters from the center of the circle. What is the length, in centimeters, of the chord?$q$, $q$["80","64","160","128"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center to a chord bisects the chord. This forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 82 (the radius) and one leg 18 (the distance to the chord). The other leg is √(82² − 18²) = √6400 = 80, which is half the chord. The chord has length 2(80) = 160.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with center O, central angle AOB has a measure of 2π/3 radians. The length of arc AB is 24π units. What is the area, in square units, of sector AOB?$q$, $q$["864π","432π","1296π","216π"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Arc length = rθ, so 24π = r(2π/3), giving r = 36. Sector area = ½r²θ = ½(36)²(2π/3) = 432π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, segment DE connects point D on AB to point E on AC, and DE is parallel to BC. If AD = 6, DE = 90, and BC = 135, what is the length of DB?$q$, $q$["3","9","6","45"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Triangles ADE and ABC are similar, so AD/AB = DE/BC = 90/135. Then AB = 6·135/90 = 9, and DB = AB − AD = 9 − 6 = 3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 35/12. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 2 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of tan(T)?$q$, $q$["35/12","24/35","37/35","12/35"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so tan(T) = tan(Q), and the scale factor 2 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 35/12 means the side opposite P is 35k and the side adjacent to P is 12k, with hypotenuse 37k (since 35² + 12² = 37²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 12k and the side adjacent to Q is 35k. Therefore tan(Q) = 12/35.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a right circular cone (including the radius and the height) is decreased by 10%. By what percent does the surface area of the right circular cone decrease?$q$, $q$["20%","10%","19%","27.1%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 0.9, area is multiplied by the square of that factor: 0.9<sup>2</sup> = 0.81. So the new surface area is 81% of the original, a decrease of 19%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$An equilateral triangle has a height of 9√3 centimeters. What is the area, in square centimeters, of the triangle?$q$, $q$["162√3","81√3","81","243"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The height of an equilateral triangle with side s is (s√3)/2. So (s√3)/2 = 9√3, giving s = 18. The area is ½·base·height = ½(18)(9√3) = 81√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x − 1)² + (y + 5)² = 25<br><br>The point (−2, −1) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the slope of line t?$q$, $q$["3/4","−4/3","−3/4","4/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (1, −5). The radius to (−2, −1) has slope (−1 − (−5))/(−2 − 1) = −4/3. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is 3/4.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A right circular cone and a right circular cylinder have the same base radius and the same height. The volume of the cylinder is 144π cubic inches. What is the volume, in cubic inches, of the space inside the cylinder but outside the cone?$q$, $q$["48π","144π","72π","96π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A cone has one-third the volume of a cylinder with the same base and height, so the cone's volume is 48π. The remaining space is 144π − 48π = 96π cubic inches.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (3x − 5)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (2x − 13)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["58","302","29","151"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 2x − 13 = ½(3x − 5). Multiplying by 2: 4x − 26 = 3x − 5, so x = 21 and x = 21. Then angle ABC = 2(21) − 13 = 29°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = 3x + 24 and y = −3x − 6 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["54","27","75","36"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where 3x + 24 = −3x − 6, at (−5, 9), so the triangle's height is |9| = 9. The x-intercepts are x = −8 and x = −2, so the base is 6. The area is ½(6)(9) = 27.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In isosceles triangle ABC, AB = AC. Side BC is extended past C to point D, and the measure of angle ACD is 135°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BAC?$q$, $q$["90","45","100","135"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angle ACB is supplementary to angle ACD, so angle ACB = 180° − 135° = 45°. Since AB = AC, the base angles are equal: angle ABC = 45°. Then angle BAC = 180° − 2(45°) = 90°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant IV. An equation of the circle is x² + y² − 6x + 24y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["153","141","−144","144"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (3, −12) and r² = 9 + 144 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |3| = 3. So 9 = 153 − c, giving c = 144. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 30, and BC = 16. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of CD?$q$, $q$["128/17","450/17","240/17","23"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(30² + 16²) = 34. The area of the triangle can be computed two ways: ½(16)(30) = ½(34)(CD). So CD = (16)(30)/34 = 240/17.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the value of cos(17π/6)?$q$, $q$["√3/2","−√3/2","1/2","−1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$17π/6 radians is 510°, which is coterminal with 150°, which lies in quadrant II. Its reference angle is 30°, so |cos| = √3/2. In this quadrant cos is negative, so the value is −√3/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar cones have surface areas in the ratio 16 : 25. The volume of the smaller one is 320 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["625","500","400","820"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 16 : 25 means k = 5/4. So the larger volume is 320·(5/4)³ = 320·125/64 = 625.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$Each interior angle of a regular polygon has a measure that is 13/2 times the measure of each of its exterior angles. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["15/2","17","14","15"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$For a regular n-gon, each exterior angle is 360°/n and each interior angle is 180° − 360°/n. Setting 180 − 360/n = 13/2(360/n) gives 180 = 15/2(360/n), so n = 15/2(360)/180 = 15.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle B measures 45°, angle C measures 30°, and AC = 20. What is the length of AB?$q$, $q$["20√2","10√3","10√2","10"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Draw altitude AD to side BC. In 30°-60°-90° triangle ADC, AD is opposite the 30° angle, so AD = ½(AC) = 10. In 45°-45°-90° triangle ABD, the hypotenuse AB = AD·√2 = 10√2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (−1, −4) and (6, k) is 25. If k > −4, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−28","20","21","24"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (6 − (−1))² + (k − (−4))² = 25², so 49 + (k − (−4))² = 625. Then (k − (−4))² = 576, so k − (−4) = ±24. Since k > −4, k = −4 + 24 = 20.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A straight ramp 50 meters long rises from level ground to a platform 14 meters high. Let θ be the angle between the ramp and the ground. What is the value of tan(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["24/7","7/24","7/25","24/25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(50² − 14²) = 48. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (14) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (48). So tan(90° − θ) = 48/14 = 24/7, which equals 1/tan(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 8x + y² − 8y − 68 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the length of the diameter of the circle?$q$, $q$["4√17","10","200","20"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² − 8x + 16) + (y² − 8y + 16) = 68 + 16 + 16, so (x − 4)² + (y − 4)² = 100. The radius is √100 = 10, so the diameter is 20.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 5 centimeters. A chord of the circle is 4 centimeters from the center of the circle. What is the length, in centimeters, of the chord?$q$, $q$["3","1","6","2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center to a chord bisects the chord. This forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 5 (the radius) and one leg 4 (the distance to the chord). The other leg is √(5² − 4²) = √9 = 3, which is half the chord. The chord has length 2(3) = 6.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with center O, central angle AOB has a measure of 5π/4 radians. The length of arc AB is 35π units. What is the area, in square units, of sector AOB?$q$, $q$["980π","490π","784π","245π"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Arc length = rθ, so 35π = r(5π/4), giving r = 28. Sector area = ½r²θ = ½(28)²(5π/4) = 490π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, point D lies on side AB and point E lies on side AC such that segment DE is parallel to side BC. If AD = 3, DB = 9, and DE = 36, what is the length of BC?$q$, $q$["144","108","12","45"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC with ratio AD/AB = 3/12. So DE/BC = 3/12, and BC = 36·12/3 = 144. A common error is to use AD/DB instead of AD/AB.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 21/20. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 5 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of cos(T)?$q$, $q$["20/29","105/29","29/21","21/29"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so cos(T) = cos(Q), and the scale factor 5 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 21/20 means the side opposite P is 21k and the side adjacent to P is 20k, with hypotenuse 29k (since 21² + 20² = 29²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 20k and the side adjacent to Q is 21k. Therefore cos(Q) = 21/29.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a cube is decreased by 40%. By what percent does the surface area of the cube decrease?$q$, $q$["80%","40%","64%","78.4%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 0.6, area is multiplied by the square of that factor: 0.6<sup>2</sup> = 0.36. So the new surface area is 36% of the original, a decrease of 64%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$An equilateral triangle has an area of 100√3 square inches. What is the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle?$q$, $q$["20","60","30","300"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The area of an equilateral triangle with side s is (√3/4)s². Setting (√3/4)s² = 100√3 gives s² = 400, so s = 20 and the perimeter is 3(20) = 60.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x + 2)² + (y + 6)² = 1369<br><br>The point (10, 29) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the slope of line t?$q$, $q$["−12/35","35/12","12/35","−35/12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (−2, −6). The radius to (10, 29) has slope (29 − (−6))/(10 − (−2)) = 35/12. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is −12/35.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A container shaped like a right circular cone is held with its vertex pointing down. It is filled with water to 2/3 of its height, measured from the vertex. The water's surface is a circle parallel to the cone's base. What fraction of the cone's volume is filled with water?$q$, $q$["2/3","4/9","8/9","8/27"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The water forms a smaller cone similar to the whole cone, with scale factor 2/3 (the ratio of heights). Volumes of similar solids scale by the cube of the scale factor, so the water fills (2/3)³ = 8/27 of the cone.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (4x − 10)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (4x − 55)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["90","270","45","135"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 4x − 55 = ½(4x − 10). Multiplying by 2: 8x − 110 = 4x − 10, so 4x = 100 and x = 25. Then angle ABC = 4(25) − 55 = 45°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = −x + 4 and y = 4x + 24 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["80","40","43","48"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where −x + 4 = 4x + 24, at (−4, 8), so the triangle's height is |8| = 8. The x-intercepts are x = 4 and x = −6, so the base is 10. The area is ½(10)(8) = 40.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle A is 138°. The bisectors of angles B and C intersect at point P. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BPC?$q$, $q$["159","42","111","21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angles B and C together measure 180° − 138° = 42°. Their halves add to 21°. In triangle BPC, angle BPC = 180° − 21° = 159°. (In general, angle BPC = 90° + A/2.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant II. An equation of the circle is x² + y² + 8x − 24y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["160","140","−144","144"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (−4, 12) and r² = 16 + 144 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |−4| = 4. So 16 = 160 − c, giving c = 144. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 120, and BC = 27. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of BD?$q$, $q$["4800/41","1080/41","243/41","27/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(120² + 27²) = 123. Triangle CBD is similar to triangle ABC (they share angle B), so BD/BC = BC/AB, giving BD = 27²/123 = 243/41.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the terminal side of an angle of 11π/4 radians in standard position intersects the unit circle at point P. What is the y-coordinate of P?$q$, $q$["−√2/2","√2/2","1/2","√3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$11π/4 radians is 495°, which is coterminal with 135°, which lies in quadrant II. Its reference angle is 45°, so |sin| = √2/2. In this quadrant sin is positive, so the value is √2/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar pyramids have surface areas in the ratio 16 : 25. The volume of the smaller one is 256 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["500","400","320","656"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 16 : 25 means k = 5/4. So the larger volume is 256·(5/4)³ = 256·125/64 = 500.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$For a certain regular polygon, the measure of each interior angle is 140° greater than the measure of each exterior angle. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["3","16","19","18"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let each exterior angle be e, so each interior angle is 180° − e. Then (180 − e) − e = 140, so e = 20°. Since the exterior angles of any convex polygon add to 360°, n = 360/20 = 18.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle B is 45° and the measure of angle C is 30°. The altitude from A to side BC has length 6. What is the length of side BC?$q$, $q$["12√3","6 + 6√2","6 + 6√3","12 + 6√3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let D be the foot of the altitude. Triangle ABD is a 45°-45°-90° triangle, so BD = AD = 6. Triangle ADC is a 30°-60°-90° triangle with the side opposite 30° equal to AD = 6, so DC = 6√3. Therefore BC = BD + DC = 6 + 6√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (−6, −4) and (−20, k) is 50. If k > −4, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−52","44","46","48"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (−20 − (−6))² + (k − (−4))² = 50², so 196 + (k − (−4))² = 2500. Then (k − (−4))² = 2304, so k − (−4) = ±48. Since k > −4, k = −4 + 48 = 44.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A straight ramp 61 meters long rises from level ground to a platform 11 meters high. Let θ be the angle between the ramp and the ground. What is the value of tan(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["60/11","11/60","11/61","60/61"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(61² − 11²) = 60. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (11) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (60). So tan(90° − θ) = 60/11 = 60/11, which equals 1/tan(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 4x + y² + 6y − 68 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the length of the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["2√17","81","√13","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² + 4x + 4) + (y² + 6y + 9) = 68 + 4 + 9, so (x + 2)² + (y + 3)² = 81. The radius is √81 = 9.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with radius 111 inches, a chord has length 72 inches. What is the distance, in inches, from the center of the circle to the chord?$q$, $q$["75","84","105","36"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center bisects the chord, so it forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 111 and leg 72/2 = 36. The distance is √(111² − 36²) = √11025 = 105.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A sector of a circle has a central angle of 5π/3 radians and an area of 480π square units. What is the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["576","24","12","288"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Sector area = ½r²θ, so 480π = ½r²(5π/3). Then r² = 2(480π)/(5π/3) = 576, so r = 24.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, segment DE connects point D on AB to point E on AC, and DE is parallel to BC. If AD = 12, DE = 180, and BC = 270, what is the length of DB?$q$, $q$["6","18","12","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Triangles ADE and ABC are similar, so AD/AB = DE/BC = 180/270. Then AB = 12·270/180 = 18, and DB = AB − AD = 18 − 12 = 6.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 12/35. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 4 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of tan(T)?$q$, $q$["12/35","35/3","37/12","35/12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so tan(T) = tan(Q), and the scale factor 4 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 12/35 means the side opposite P is 12k and the side adjacent to P is 35k, with hypotenuse 37k (since 12² + 35² = 37²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 35k and the side adjacent to Q is 12k. Therefore tan(Q) = 35/12.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a cube is increased by 20%. By what percent does the surface area of the cube increase?$q$, $q$["40%","20%","44%","72.8%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 1.2, area is multiplied by the square of that factor: 1.2<sup>2</sup> = 1.44. So the new surface area is 144% of the original, an increase of 44%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$An equilateral triangle has an area of 64√3 square inches. What is the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle?$q$, $q$["16","48","24","192"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The area of an equilateral triangle with side s is (√3/4)s². Setting (√3/4)s² = 64√3 gives s² = 256, so s = 16 and the perimeter is 3(16) = 48.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x − 2)² + (y − 1)² = 1681<br><br>The point (11, −39) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the slope of line t?$q$, $q$["9/40","−40/9","−9/40","40/9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (2, 1). The radius to (11, −39) has slope (−39 − 1)/(11 − 2) = −40/9. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is 9/40.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A container shaped like a right circular cone is held with its vertex pointing down. It is filled with water to 1/4 of its height, measured from the vertex. The water's surface is a circle parallel to the cone's base. What fraction of the cone's volume is filled with water?$q$, $q$["1/4","1/16","1/8","1/64"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The water forms a smaller cone similar to the whole cone, with scale factor 1/4 (the ratio of heights). Volumes of similar solids scale by the cube of the scale factor, so the water fills (1/4)³ = 1/64 of the cone.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (5x − 13)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (4x − 38)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["92","268","46","134"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 4x − 38 = ½(5x − 13). Multiplying by 2: 8x − 76 = 5x − 13, so 3x = 63 and x = 21. Then angle ABC = 4(21) − 38 = 46°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = 4x and y = 2x − 4 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["16","8","4","9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where 4x = 2x − 4, at (−2, −8), so the triangle's height is |−8| = 8. The x-intercepts are x = 0 and x = 2, so the base is 2. The area is ½(2)(8) = 8.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In isosceles triangle ABC, AB = AC. Side BC is extended past C to point D, and the measure of angle ACD is 159°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BAC?$q$, $q$["138","21","148","159"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angle ACB is supplementary to angle ACD, so angle ACB = 180° − 159° = 21°. Since AB = AC, the base angles are equal: angle ABC = 21°. Then angle BAC = 180° − 2(21°) = 138°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant II. An equation of the circle is x² + y² + 10x − 6y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["34","4","−9","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (−5, 3) and r² = 25 + 9 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |−5| = 5. So 25 = 34 − c, giving c = 9. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 8, and BC = 6. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of CD?$q$, $q$["18/5","32/5","24/5","7"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(8² + 6²) = 10. The area of the triangle can be computed two ways: ½(6)(8) = ½(10)(CD). So CD = (6)(8)/10 = 24/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the value of sin(8π/3)?$q$, $q$["−√3/2","√3/2","−1/2","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$8π/3 radians is 480°, which is coterminal with 120°, which lies in quadrant II. Its reference angle is 60°, so |sin| = √3/2. In this quadrant sin is positive, so the value is √3/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar cones have surface areas in the ratio 9 : 25. The volume of the smaller one is 162 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["750","450","270","612"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 9 : 25 means k = 5/3. So the larger volume is 162·(5/3)³ = 162·125/27 = 750.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$For a certain regular polygon, the measure of each interior angle is 144° greater than the measure of each exterior angle. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["3","18","21","20"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let each exterior angle be e, so each interior angle is 180° − e. Then (180 − e) − e = 144, so e = 18°. Since the exterior angles of any convex polygon add to 360°, n = 360/18 = 20.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h58$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle B is 45° and the measure of angle C is 30°. The altitude from A to side BC has length 9. What is the length of side BC?$q$, $q$["18√3","9 + 9√2","9 + 9√3","18 + 9√3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let D be the foot of the altitude. Triangle ABD is a 45°-45°-90° triangle, so BD = AD = 9. Triangle ADC is a 30°-60°-90° triangle with the side opposite 30° equal to AD = 9, so DC = 9√3. Therefore BC = BD + DC = 9 + 9√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (4, −4) and (−16, k) is 29. If k > −4, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−25","17","25","21"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (−16 − 4)² + (k − (−4))² = 29², so 400 + (k − (−4))² = 841. Then (k − (−4))² = 441, so k − (−4) = ±21. Since k > −4, k = −4 + 21 = 17.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h60$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A 10-foot ladder leans against a vertical wall. The top of the ladder touches the wall 6 feet above the level ground. Let θ be the angle between the ladder and the ground. What is the value of cos(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["3/5","4/5","3/4","5/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(10² − 6²) = 8. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (6) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (8). So cos(90° − θ) = 6/10 = 3/5, which equals sin(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h61$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 16x + y² − 8y + 76 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the length of the diameter of the circle?$q$, $q$["4√19","2","8","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² + 16x + 64) + (y² − 8y + 16) = −76 + 64 + 16, so (x + 8)² + (y − 4)² = 4. The radius is √4 = 2, so the diameter is 4.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h62$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 25 centimeters. A chord of the circle is 7 centimeters from the center of the circle. What is the length, in centimeters, of the chord?$q$, $q$["24","18","48","36"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center to a chord bisects the chord. This forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 25 (the radius) and one leg 7 (the distance to the chord). The other leg is √(25² − 7²) = √576 = 24, which is half the chord. The chord has length 2(24) = 48.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h63$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with center O, central angle AOB has a measure of 2π/3 radians. The length of arc AB is 4π units. What is the area, in square units, of sector AOB?$q$, $q$["24π","12π","36π","6π"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Arc length = rθ, so 4π = r(2π/3), giving r = 6. Sector area = ½r²θ = ½(6)²(2π/3) = 12π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h64$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, point D lies on side AB and point E lies on side AC such that segment DE is parallel to side BC. If AD = 2, DB = 10, and DE = 18, what is the length of BC?$q$, $q$["108","90","18/5","28"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC with ratio AD/AB = 2/12. So DE/BC = 2/12, and BC = 18·12/2 = 108. A common error is to use AD/DB instead of AD/AB.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h65$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 11/60. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 2 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of sin(T)?$q$, $q$["11/61","120/61","61/60","60/61"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so sin(T) = sin(Q), and the scale factor 2 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 11/60 means the side opposite P is 11k and the side adjacent to P is 60k, with hypotenuse 61k (since 11² + 60² = 61²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 60k and the side adjacent to Q is 11k. Therefore sin(Q) = 60/61.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h66$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a right circular cone (including the radius and the height) is decreased by 40%. By what percent does the surface area of the right circular cone decrease?$q$, $q$["80%","40%","64%","78.4%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 0.6, area is multiplied by the square of that factor: 0.6<sup>2</sup> = 0.36. So the new surface area is 36% of the original, a decrease of 64%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h67$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$An equilateral triangle has an area of 49√3 square inches. What is the perimeter, in inches, of the triangle?$q$, $q$["14","42","21","147"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The area of an equilateral triangle with side s is (√3/4)s². Setting (√3/4)s² = 49√3 gives s² = 196, so s = 14 and the perimeter is 3(14) = 42.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h68$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x − 1)² + (y − 1)² = 169<br><br>The point (6, −11) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the slope of line t?$q$, $q$["5/12","−12/5","−5/12","12/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (1, 1). The radius to (6, −11) has slope (−11 − 1)/(6 − 1) = −12/5. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is 5/12.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h69$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A right circular cone and a right circular cylinder have the same base radius and the same height. The volume of the cylinder is 36π cubic inches. What is the volume, in cubic inches, of the space inside the cylinder but outside the cone?$q$, $q$["12π","36π","18π","24π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A cone has one-third the volume of a cylinder with the same base and height, so the cone's volume is 12π. The remaining space is 36π − 12π = 24π cubic inches.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h70$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (5x + 5)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (3x − 10)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["130","230","65","115"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 3x − 10 = ½(5x + 5). Multiplying by 2: 6x − 20 = 5x + 5, so x = 25 and x = 25. Then angle ABC = 3(25) − 10 = 65°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h71$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = −4x − 34 and y = −2x − 22 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["25","25/2","36","45/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where −4x − 34 = −2x − 22, at (−6, −10), so the triangle's height is |−10| = 10. The x-intercepts are x = −17/2 and x = −11, so the base is 5/2. The area is ½(5/2)(10) = 25/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h72$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle A is 72°. The bisectors of angles B and C intersect at point P. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BPC?$q$, $q$["126","108","144","54"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angles B and C together measure 180° − 72° = 108°. Their halves add to 54°. In triangle BPC, angle BPC = 180° − 54° = 126°. (In general, angle BPC = 90° + A/2.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h73$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant IV. An equation of the circle is x² + y² − 12x + 12y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["72","30","−36","36"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (6, −6) and r² = 36 + 36 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |6| = 6. So 36 = 72 − c, giving c = 36. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h74$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 80, and BC = 18. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of BD?$q$, $q$["3200/41","720/41","162/41","9"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(80² + 18²) = 82. Triangle CBD is similar to triangle ABC (they share angle B), so BD/BC = BC/AB, giving BD = 18²/82 = 162/41.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h75$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the value of sin(2π/3)?$q$, $q$["−√3/2","√3/2","−1/2","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$2π/3 radians is 120°, which lies in quadrant II. Its reference angle is 60°, so |sin| = √3/2. In this quadrant sin is positive, so the value is √3/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h76$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar cylinders have surface areas in the ratio 1 : 9. The volume of the smaller one is 3 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["81","27","9","30"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 1 : 9 means k = 3/1. So the larger volume is 3·(3/1)³ = 3·27/1 = 81.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h77$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a regular polygon is 3240°. What is the measure, in degrees, of each exterior angle of the polygon?$q$, $q$["162","9","20","18"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The interior angles of an n-gon add to (n − 2)·180°, so (n − 2)·180 = 3240 and n = 20. Each exterior angle of a regular 20-gon is 360°/20 = 18°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h78$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle B measures 45°, angle C measures 30°, and AC = 6. What is the length of AB?$q$, $q$["6√2","3√3","3√2","3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Draw altitude AD to side BC. In 30°-60°-90° triangle ADC, AD is opposite the 30° angle, so AD = ½(AC) = 3. In 45°-45°-90° triangle ABD, the hypotenuse AB = AD·√2 = 3√2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h79$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (−4, −8) and (−1, k) is 5. If k > −8, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−12","−4","−3","4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (−1 − (−4))² + (k − (−8))² = 5², so 9 + (k − (−8))² = 25. Then (k − (−8))² = 16, so k − (−8) = ±4. Since k > −8, k = −8 + 4 = −4.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h80$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A straight ramp 58 meters long rises from level ground to a platform 40 meters high. Let θ be the angle between the ramp and the ground. What is the value of cos(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["20/29","21/29","20/21","29/20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(58² − 40²) = 42. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (40) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (42). So cos(90° − θ) = 40/58 = 20/29, which equals sin(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h81$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 12x + y² − 4y + 28 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the area of the circle?$q$, $q$["28π","144π","24π","12π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² + 12x + 36) + (y² − 4y + 4) = −28 + 36 + 4, so (x + 6)² + (y − 2)² = 12. The radius is √12 = 2√3, so the area is π(12) = 12π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h82$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with radius 75 inches, a chord has length 144 inches. What is the distance, in inches, from the center of the circle to the chord?$q$, $q$["3","73","21","72"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center bisects the chord, so it forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 75 and leg 144/2 = 72. The distance is √(75² − 72²) = √441 = 21.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h83$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A sector of a circle has a central angle of π/4 radians and an area of 288π square units. What is the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["2304","48","24","1152"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Sector area = ½r²θ, so 288π = ½r²(π/4). Then r² = 2(288π)/(π/4) = 2304, so r = 48.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h84$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, point D lies on side AB and point E lies on side AC such that segment DE is parallel to side BC. If AD = 4, DB = 2, and DE = 44, what is the length of BC?$q$, $q$["66","22","88","46"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC with ratio AD/AB = 4/6. So DE/BC = 4/6, and BC = 44·6/4 = 66. A common error is to use AD/DB instead of AD/AB.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h85$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 5/12. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 4 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of tan(T)?$q$, $q$["5/12","48/5","13/5","12/5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so tan(T) = tan(Q), and the scale factor 4 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 5/12 means the side opposite P is 5k and the side adjacent to P is 12k, with hypotenuse 13k (since 5² + 12² = 13²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 12k and the side adjacent to Q is 5k. Therefore tan(Q) = 12/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h86$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a right circular cone (including the radius and the height) is decreased by 50%. By what percent does the volume of the right circular cone decrease?$q$, $q$["150%","50%","87.5%","75%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 0.5, volume is multiplied by the cube of that factor: 0.5<sup>3</sup> = 0.125. So the new volume is 12.5% of the original, a decrease of 87.5%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h87$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$A regular hexagon has a perimeter of 72 meters. What is the area, in square meters, of the hexagon?$q$, $q$["36√3","216√3","108√3","432√3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Each side is 72/6 = 12. A regular hexagon is made of 6 equilateral triangles with side 12, each with area (√3/4)(12)² = 36√3. The total area is 6 × 36√3 = 216√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h88$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x − 1)² + (y + 3)² = 3721<br><br>The point (12, 57) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of line t?$q$, $q$["296/5","−93/11","274/5","−169/60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (1, −3). The radius to (12, 57) has slope (57 − (−3))/(12 − 1) = 60/11. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is −11/60. Using y − 57 = −11/60(x − 12) and setting x = 0 gives y = 296/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h89$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A sphere is placed inside a right circular cylinder so that the sphere touches the top, the bottom, and the curved side of the cylinder. The cylinder has a volume of 1458π cubic centimeters. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the sphere?$q$, $q$["486π","1944π","729π","972π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If the sphere has radius R, the cylinder has radius R and height 2R, so its volume is πR²(2R) = 2πR³ = 1458π. Then R³ = 729 and R = 9. The sphere's volume is (4/3)πR³ = (4/3)π(729) = 972π. (In general, the sphere fills 2/3 of the cylinder.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h90$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (4x + 20)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (3x − 11)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["104","256","52","128"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 3x − 11 = ½(4x + 20). Multiplying by 2: 6x − 22 = 4x + 20, so 2x = 42 and x = 21. Then angle ABC = 3(21) − 11 = 52°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h91$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = 4x + 18 and y = −3x − 24 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["21","21/2","126","33/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where 4x + 18 = −3x − 24, at (−6, −6), so the triangle's height is |−6| = 6. The x-intercepts are x = −9/2 and x = −8, so the base is 7/2. The area is ½(7/2)(6) = 21/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h92$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle A is 156°. The bisectors of angles B and C intersect at point P. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BPC?$q$, $q$["168","24","102","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angles B and C together measure 180° − 156° = 24°. Their halves add to 12°. In triangle BPC, angle BPC = 180° − 12° = 168°. (In general, angle BPC = 90° + A/2.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h93$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant IV. An equation of the circle is x² + y² − 18x + 14y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["130","40","−49","49"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (9, −7) and r² = 81 + 49 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |9| = 9. So 81 = 130 − c, giving c = 49. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h94$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 21, and BC = 20. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of CD?$q$, $q$["400/29","441/29","420/29","41/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(21² + 20²) = 29. The area of the triangle can be computed two ways: ½(20)(21) = ½(29)(CD). So CD = (20)(21)/29 = 420/29.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h95$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the terminal side of an angle of 17π/6 radians in standard position intersects the unit circle at point P. What is the x-coordinate of P?$q$, $q$["√3/2","−√3/2","1/2","−1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$17π/6 radians is 510°, which is coterminal with 150°, which lies in quadrant II. Its reference angle is 30°, so |cos| = √3/2. In this quadrant cos is negative, so the value is −√3/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h96$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar cylinders have surface areas in the ratio 16 : 36. The volume of the smaller one is 320 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["1080","720","480","1040"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 16 : 36 means k = 6/4. So the larger volume is 320·(6/4)³ = 320·216/64 = 1080.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h97$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$For a certain regular polygon, the measure of each interior angle is 132° greater than the measure of each exterior angle. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["3","13","16","15"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let each exterior angle be e, so each interior angle is 180° − e. Then (180 − e) − e = 132, so e = 24°. Since the exterior angles of any convex polygon add to 360°, n = 360/24 = 15.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h98$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle B measures 45°, angle C measures 30°, and AC = 8. What is the length of AB?$q$, $q$["8√2","4√3","4√2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Draw altitude AD to side BC. In 30°-60°-90° triangle ADC, AD is opposite the 30° angle, so AD = ½(AC) = 4. In 45°-45°-90° triangle ABD, the hypotenuse AB = AD·√2 = 4√2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h99$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (3, 4) and (11, k) is 17. If k > 4, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−11","19","21","15"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (11 − 3)² + (k − 4)² = 17², so 64 + (k − 4)² = 289. Then (k − 4)² = 225, so k − 4 = ±15. Since k > 4, k = 4 + 15 = 19.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h100$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A taut 20-meter support wire runs from the top of a 12-meter vertical pole to a point on the level ground. Let θ be the angle between the wire and the ground. What is the value of cos(θ) + sin(θ)?$q$, $q$["7/5","3/5","4/5","7/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal distance is √(20² − 12²) = 16. With θ at the ground, sin(θ) = opposite/hypotenuse = 12/20 = 3/5 and cos(θ) = adjacent/hypotenuse = 16/20 = 4/5. Their sum is 7/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h101$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 14x + y² − 2y + 46 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the area of the circle?$q$, $q$["46π","16π","8π","4π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² + 14x + 49) + (y² − 2y + 1) = −46 + 49 + 1, so (x + 7)² + (y − 1)² = 4. The radius is √4 = 2, so the area is π(4) = 4π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h102$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 111 centimeters. A chord of the circle is 36 centimeters from the center of the circle. What is the length, in centimeters, of the chord?$q$, $q$["105","75","210","150"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center to a chord bisects the chord. This forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 111 (the radius) and one leg 36 (the distance to the chord). The other leg is √(111² − 36²) = √11025 = 105, which is half the chord. The chord has length 2(105) = 210.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h103$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with center O, central angle AOB has a measure of π/3 radians. The length of arc AB is 4π units. What is the area, in square units, of sector AOB?$q$, $q$["48π","24π","144π","12π"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Arc length = rθ, so 4π = r(π/3), giving r = 12. Sector area = ½r²θ = ½(12)²(π/3) = 24π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h104$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, segment DE connects point D on AB to point E on AC, and DE is parallel to BC. If AD = 7, DE = 70, and BC = 170, what is the length of DB?$q$, $q$["10","17","7","100"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Triangles ADE and ABC are similar, so AD/AB = DE/BC = 70/170. Then AB = 7·170/70 = 17, and DB = AB − AD = 17 − 7 = 10.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h105$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 12/35. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 2 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of tan(T)?$q$, $q$["12/35","35/6","37/12","35/12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so tan(T) = tan(Q), and the scale factor 2 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 12/35 means the side opposite P is 12k and the side adjacent to P is 35k, with hypotenuse 37k (since 12² + 35² = 37²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 35k and the side adjacent to Q is 12k. Therefore tan(Q) = 35/12.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h106$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a sphere is increased by 40%. By what percent does the volume of the sphere increase?$q$, $q$["120%","40%","174.4%","96%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 1.4, volume is multiplied by the cube of that factor: 1.4<sup>3</sup> = 2.744. So the new volume is 274.4% of the original, an increase of 174.4%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h107$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$A regular hexagon has a perimeter of 24 meters. What is the area, in square meters, of the hexagon?$q$, $q$["4√3","24√3","12√3","48√3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Each side is 24/6 = 4. A regular hexagon is made of 6 equilateral triangles with side 4, each with area (√3/4)(4)² = 4√3. The total area is 6 × 4√3 = 24√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h108$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 3721<br><br>The point (−9, −63) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the slope of line t?$q$, $q$["−11/60","60/11","11/60","−60/11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (2, −3). The radius to (−9, −63) has slope (−63 − (−3))/(−9 − 2) = 60/11. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is −11/60.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h109$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A right circular cone and a right circular cylinder have the same base radius and the same height. The volume of the cylinder is 686π cubic inches. What is the volume, in cubic inches, of the space inside the cylinder but outside the cone?$q$, $q$["686/3π","686π","343π","1372/3π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A cone has one-third the volume of a cylinder with the same base and height, so the cone's volume is 686/3π. The remaining space is 686π − 686/3π = 1372/3π cubic inches.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h110$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (3x + 8)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (2x − 6)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["68","292","34","146"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 2x − 6 = ½(3x + 8). Multiplying by 2: 4x − 12 = 3x + 8, so x = 20 and x = 20. Then angle ABC = 2(20) − 6 = 34°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h111$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = −4x + 10 and y = 3x − 18 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["21","21/2","56","33/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where −4x + 10 = 3x − 18, at (4, −6), so the triangle's height is |−6| = 6. The x-intercepts are x = 5/2 and x = 6, so the base is 7/2. The area is ½(7/2)(6) = 21/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h112$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle A is 146°. The bisectors of angles B and C intersect at point P. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BPC?$q$, $q$["163","34","107","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angles B and C together measure 180° − 146° = 34°. Their halves add to 17°. In triangle BPC, angle BPC = 180° − 17° = 163°. (In general, angle BPC = 90° + A/2.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h113$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant I. An equation of the circle is x² + y² − 10x − 6y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["34","4","−9","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (5, 3) and r² = 25 + 9 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |5| = 5. So 25 = 34 − c, giving c = 9. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h114$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 105, and BC = 36. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of CD?$q$, $q$["432/37","3675/37","1260/37","141/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(105² + 36²) = 111. The area of the triangle can be computed two ways: ½(36)(105) = ½(111)(CD). So CD = (36)(105)/111 = 1260/37.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h115$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the value of sin(5π/4)?$q$, $q$["√2/2","−√2/2","1/2","√3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$5π/4 radians is 225°, which lies in quadrant III. Its reference angle is 45°, so |sin| = √2/2. In this quadrant sin is negative, so the value is −√2/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h116$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar cylinders have surface areas in the ratio 16 : 25. The volume of the smaller one is 192 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["375","300","240","492"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 16 : 25 means k = 5/4. So the larger volume is 192·(5/4)³ = 192·125/64 = 375.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h117$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$For a certain regular polygon, the measure of each interior angle is 120° greater than the measure of each exterior angle. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["3","10","13","12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let each exterior angle be e, so each interior angle is 180° − e. Then (180 − e) − e = 120, so e = 30°. Since the exterior angles of any convex polygon add to 360°, n = 360/30 = 12.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h118$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle B measures 45°, angle C measures 30°, and AC = 4. What is the length of AB?$q$, $q$["4√2","2√3","2√2","2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Draw altitude AD to side BC. In 30°-60°-90° triangle ADC, AD is opposite the 30° angle, so AD = ½(AC) = 2. In 45°-45°-90° triangle ABD, the hypotenuse AB = AD·√2 = 2√2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h119$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (2, −2) and (16, k) is 50. If k > −2, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−50","46","48","60"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (16 − 2)² + (k − (−2))² = 50², so 196 + (k − (−2))² = 2500. Then (k − (−2))² = 2304, so k − (−2) = ±48. Since k > −2, k = −2 + 48 = 46.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h120$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A straight ramp 26 meters long rises from level ground to a platform 10 meters high. Let θ be the angle between the ramp and the ground. What is the value of cos(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["5/13","12/13","5/12","13/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(26² − 10²) = 24. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (10) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (24). So cos(90° − θ) = 10/26 = 5/13, which equals sin(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h121$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 10x + y² + 4y + 20 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the length of the diameter of the circle?$q$, $q$["4√5","3","18","6"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² + 10x + 25) + (y² + 4y + 4) = −20 + 25 + 4, so (x + 5)² + (y + 2)² = 9. The radius is √9 = 3, so the diameter is 6.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h122$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with radius 123 inches, a chord has length 240 inches. What is the distance, in inches, from the center of the circle to the chord?$q$, $q$["3","121","27","120"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center bisects the chord, so it forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 123 and leg 240/2 = 120. The distance is √(123² − 120²) = √729 = 27.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h123$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with center O, central angle AOB has a measure of 7π/4 radians. The length of arc AB is 42π units. What is the area, in square units, of sector AOB?$q$, $q$["1008π","504π","576π","252π"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Arc length = rθ, so 42π = r(7π/4), giving r = 24. Sector area = ½r²θ = ½(24)²(7π/4) = 504π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h124$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, point D lies on side AB and point E lies on side AC such that segment DE is parallel to side BC. If AD = 11, DB = 3, and DE = 77, what is the length of BC?$q$, $q$["98","21","847/3","80"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC with ratio AD/AB = 11/14. So DE/BC = 11/14, and BC = 77·14/11 = 98. A common error is to use AD/DB instead of AD/AB.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h125$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 5/12. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 2 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of tan(T)?$q$, $q$["5/12","24/5","13/5","12/5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so tan(T) = tan(Q), and the scale factor 2 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 5/12 means the side opposite P is 5k and the side adjacent to P is 12k, with hypotenuse 13k (since 5² + 12² = 13²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 12k and the side adjacent to Q is 5k. Therefore tan(Q) = 12/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h126$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a rectangular prism is decreased by 50%. By what percent does the volume of the rectangular prism decrease?$q$, $q$["150%","50%","87.5%","75%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 0.5, volume is multiplied by the cube of that factor: 0.5<sup>3</sup> = 0.125. So the new volume is 12.5% of the original, a decrease of 87.5%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h127$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$A regular hexagon has a perimeter of 66 meters. What is the area, in square meters, of the hexagon?$q$, $q$["30.25√3","181.5√3","90.75√3","363√3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Each side is 66/6 = 11. A regular hexagon is made of 6 equilateral triangles with side 11, each with area (√3/4)(11)² = 30.25√3. The total area is 6 × 30.25√3 = 181.5√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h128$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x + 2)² + (y + 3)² = 169<br><br>The point (3, 9) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of line t?$q$, $q$["41/4","9/5","31/4","−23/6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (−2, −3). The radius to (3, 9) has slope (9 − (−3))/(3 − (−2)) = 12/5. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is −5/12. Using y − 9 = −5/12(x − 3) and setting x = 0 gives y = 41/4.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h129$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A container shaped like a right circular cone is held with its vertex pointing down. It is filled with water to 3/4 of its height, measured from the vertex. The water's surface is a circle parallel to the cone's base. What fraction of the cone's volume is filled with water?$q$, $q$["3/4","9/16","9/8","27/64"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The water forms a smaller cone similar to the whole cone, with scale factor 3/4 (the ratio of heights). Volumes of similar solids scale by the cube of the scale factor, so the water fills (3/4)³ = 27/64 of the cone.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h130$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (5x − 12)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (2x − 1)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["38","322","19","161"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 2x − 1 = ½(5x − 12). Multiplying by 2: 4x − 2 = 5x − 12, so -x = −10 and x = 10. Then angle ABC = 2(10) − 1 = 19°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h131$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = −2x − 10 and y = −x − 4 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["2","1","18","3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where −2x − 10 = −x − 4, at (−6, 2), so the triangle's height is |2| = 2. The x-intercepts are x = −5 and x = −4, so the base is 1. The area is ½(1)(2) = 1.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h132$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle A is 104°. The bisectors of angles B and C intersect at point P. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BPC?$q$, $q$["142","76","128","38"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angles B and C together measure 180° − 104° = 76°. Their halves add to 38°. In triangle BPC, angle BPC = 180° − 38° = 142°. (In general, angle BPC = 90° + A/2.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h133$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant II. An equation of the circle is x² + y² + 6x − 16y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["73","61","−64","64"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (−3, 8) and r² = 9 + 64 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |−3| = 3. So 9 = 73 − c, giving c = 64. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h134$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 12, and BC = 5. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of BD?$q$, $q$["144/13","60/13","25/13","5/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(12² + 5²) = 13. Triangle CBD is similar to triangle ABC (they share angle B), so BD/BC = BC/AB, giving BD = 5²/13 = 25/13.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h135$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the value of sin(7π/6)?$q$, $q$["1/2","−1/2","−√3/2","√3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$7π/6 radians is 210°, which lies in quadrant III. Its reference angle is 30°, so |sin| = 1/2. In this quadrant sin is negative, so the value is −1/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h136$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar cylinders have surface areas in the ratio 1 : 9. The volume of the smaller one is 4 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["108","36","12","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 1 : 9 means k = 3/1. So the larger volume is 4·(3/1)³ = 4·27/1 = 108.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h137$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$For a certain regular polygon, the measure of each interior angle is 90° greater than the measure of each exterior angle. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["4","6","9","8"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let each exterior angle be e, so each interior angle is 180° − e. Then (180 − e) − e = 90, so e = 45°. Since the exterior angles of any convex polygon add to 360°, n = 360/45 = 8.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h138$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle B measures 45°, angle C measures 30°, and AC = 10. What is the length of AB?$q$, $q$["10√2","5√3","5√2","5"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Draw altitude AD to side BC. In 30°-60°-90° triangle ADC, AD is opposite the 30° angle, so AD = ½(AC) = 5. In 45°-45°-90° triangle ABD, the hypotenuse AB = AD·√2 = 5√2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h139$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (−8, 2) and (2, k) is 26. If k > 2, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−22","26","28","24"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (2 − (−8))² + (k − 2)² = 26², so 100 + (k − 2)² = 676. Then (k − 2)² = 576, so k − 2 = ±24. Since k > 2, k = 2 + 24 = 26.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h140$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A 17-foot ladder leans against a vertical wall. The top of the ladder touches the wall 8 feet above the level ground. Let θ be the angle between the ladder and the ground. What is the value of tan(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["15/8","8/15","8/17","15/17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(17² − 8²) = 15. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (8) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (15). So tan(90° − θ) = 15/8 = 15/8, which equals 1/tan(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h141$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 6x + y² + 16y + 57 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the length of the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["√57","16","√73","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² − 6x + 9) + (y² + 16y + 64) = −57 + 9 + 64, so (x − 3)² + (y + 8)² = 16. The radius is √16 = 4.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h142$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 15 centimeters. A chord of the circle is 9 centimeters from the center of the circle. What is the length, in centimeters, of the chord?$q$, $q$["12","6","24","25"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center to a chord bisects the chord. This forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 15 (the radius) and one leg 9 (the distance to the chord). The other leg is √(15² − 9²) = √144 = 12, which is half the chord. The chord has length 2(12) = 24.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h143$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A sector of a circle has a central angle of 4π/5 radians and an area of 90π square units. What is the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["225","15","8","30"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Sector area = ½r²θ, so 90π = ½r²(4π/5). Then r² = 2(90π)/(4π/5) = 225, so r = 15.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h144$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, point D lies on side AB and point E lies on side AC such that segment DE is parallel to side BC. If AD = 10, DB = 6, and DE = 40, what is the length of BC?$q$, $q$["64","24","200/3","46"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC with ratio AD/AB = 10/16. So DE/BC = 10/16, and BC = 40·16/10 = 64. A common error is to use AD/DB instead of AD/AB.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h145$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 8/15. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 5 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of sin(T)?$q$, $q$["8/17","75/17","17/15","15/17"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so sin(T) = sin(Q), and the scale factor 5 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 8/15 means the side opposite P is 8k and the side adjacent to P is 15k, with hypotenuse 17k (since 8² + 15² = 17²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 15k and the side adjacent to Q is 8k. Therefore sin(Q) = 15/17.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h146$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a right circular cone (including the radius and the height) is decreased by 20%. By what percent does the volume of the right circular cone decrease?$q$, $q$["60%","20%","48.8%","36%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 0.8, volume is multiplied by the cube of that factor: 0.8<sup>3</sup> = 0.512. So the new volume is 51.2% of the original, a decrease of 48.8%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h147$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$An equilateral triangle has a height of 7√3 centimeters. What is the area, in square centimeters, of the triangle?$q$, $q$["98√3","49√3","49","147"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The height of an equilateral triangle with side s is (s√3)/2. So (s√3)/2 = 7√3, giving s = 14. The area is ½·base·height = ½(14)(7√3) = 49√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h148$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x + 2)² + (y − 2)² = 1369<br><br>The point (−14, 37) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of line t?$q$, $q$["209/5","−23/6","161/5","94/35"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (−2, 2). The radius to (−14, 37) has slope (37 − 2)/(−14 − (−2)) = −35/12. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is 12/35. Using y − 37 = 12/35(x − (−14)) and setting x = 0 gives y = 209/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h149$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A right circular cone and a right circular cylinder have the same base radius and the same height. The volume of the cylinder is 72π cubic inches. What is the volume, in cubic inches, of the space inside the cylinder but outside the cone?$q$, $q$["24π","72π","36π","48π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A cone has one-third the volume of a cylinder with the same base and height, so the cone's volume is 24π. The remaining space is 72π − 24π = 48π cubic inches.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h150$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (4x − 8)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (3x − 14)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["32","328","16","164"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 3x − 14 = ½(4x − 8). Multiplying by 2: 6x − 28 = 4x − 8, so 2x = 20 and x = 10. Then angle ABC = 3(10) − 14 = 16°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h151$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = −4x + 18 and y = 3x − 24 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["21","21/2","126","33/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where −4x + 18 = 3x − 24, at (6, −6), so the triangle's height is |−6| = 6. The x-intercepts are x = 9/2 and x = 8, so the base is 7/2. The area is ½(7/2)(6) = 21/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h152$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the bisectors of angles B and C intersect at point P inside the triangle. If the measure of angle BPC is 137°, what is the measure, in degrees, of angle A?$q$, $q$["94","43","86","47"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let angle B = 2b and angle C = 2c. In triangle BPC, angle BPC = 180° − b − c. Since 2b + 2c = 180° − A, b + c = 90° − A/2, so angle BPC = 90° + A/2. Then 137 = 90 + A/2 gives A = 94°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h153$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant III. An equation of the circle is x² + y² + 18x + 14y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["130","40","−49","49"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (−9, −7) and r² = 81 + 49 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |−9| = 9. So 81 = 130 − c, giving c = 49. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h154$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 12, and BC = 5. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of CD?$q$, $q$["25/13","144/13","60/13","17/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(12² + 5²) = 13. The area of the triangle can be computed two ways: ½(5)(12) = ½(13)(CD). So CD = (5)(12)/13 = 60/13.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h155$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the value of sin(4π/3)?$q$, $q$["√3/2","−√3/2","−1/2","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$4π/3 radians is 240°, which lies in quadrant III. Its reference angle is 60°, so |sin| = √3/2. In this quadrant sin is negative, so the value is −√3/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h156$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar rectangular prisms have surface areas in the ratio 1 : 36. The volume of the smaller one is 6 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["1296","216","36","222"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 1 : 36 means k = 6/1. So the larger volume is 6·(6/1)³ = 6·216/1 = 1296.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h157$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$Each interior angle of a regular polygon has a measure that is 17 times the measure of each of its exterior angles. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["18","38","72","36"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$For a regular n-gon, each exterior angle is 360°/n and each interior angle is 180° − 360°/n. Setting 180 − 360/n = 17(360/n) gives 180 = 18(360/n), so n = 18(360)/180 = 36.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h158$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle B is 45° and the measure of angle C is 30°. The altitude from A to side BC has length 2. What is the length of side BC?$q$, $q$["4√3","2 + 2√2","2 + 2√3","4 + 2√3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let D be the foot of the altitude. Triangle ABD is a 45°-45°-90° triangle, so BD = AD = 2. Triangle ADC is a 30°-60°-90° triangle with the side opposite 30° equal to AD = 2, so DC = 2√3. Therefore BC = BD + DC = 2 + 2√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h159$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (−5, −6) and (−12, k) is 25. If k > −6, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−30","18","19","24"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (−12 − (−5))² + (k − (−6))² = 25², so 49 + (k − (−6))² = 625. Then (k − (−6))² = 576, so k − (−6) = ±24. Since k > −6, k = −6 + 24 = 18.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h160$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A 29-foot ladder leans against a vertical wall. The top of the ladder touches the wall 20 feet above the level ground. Let θ be the angle between the ladder and the ground. What is the value of tan(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["21/20","20/21","20/29","21/29"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(29² − 20²) = 21. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (20) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (21). So tan(90° − θ) = 21/20 = 21/20, which equals 1/tan(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h161$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 4x + y² − 16y + 41 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the area of the circle?$q$, $q$["41π","729π","54π","27π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² − 4x + 4) + (y² − 16y + 64) = −41 + 4 + 64, so (x − 2)² + (y − 8)² = 27. The radius is √27 = 3√3, so the area is π(27) = 27π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h162$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with radius 50 inches, a chord has length 28 inches. What is the distance, in inches, from the center of the circle to the chord?$q$, $q$["36","41","48","14"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center bisects the chord, so it forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 50 and leg 28/2 = 14. The distance is √(50² − 14²) = √2304 = 48.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h163$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with center O, central angle AOB has a measure of 3π/2 radians. The length of arc AB is 6π units. What is the area, in square units, of sector AOB?$q$, $q$["24π","12π","16π","6π"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Arc length = rθ, so 6π = r(3π/2), giving r = 4. Sector area = ½r²θ = ½(4)²(3π/2) = 12π.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h164$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, segment DE connects point D on AB to point E on AC, and DE is parallel to BC. If AD = 2, DE = 20, and BC = 140, what is the length of DB?$q$, $q$["12","14","2","120"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Triangles ADE and ABC are similar, so AD/AB = DE/BC = 20/140. Then AB = 2·140/20 = 14, and DB = AB − AD = 14 − 2 = 12.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h165$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 3/4. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 5 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of cos(T)?$q$, $q$["4/5","3","5/3","3/5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so cos(T) = cos(Q), and the scale factor 5 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 3/4 means the side opposite P is 3k and the side adjacent to P is 4k, with hypotenuse 5k (since 3² + 4² = 5²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 4k and the side adjacent to Q is 3k. Therefore cos(Q) = 3/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h166$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a rectangular prism is decreased by 30%. By what percent does the surface area of the rectangular prism decrease?$q$, $q$["60%","30%","51%","65.7%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 0.7, area is multiplied by the square of that factor: 0.7<sup>2</sup> = 0.49. So the new surface area is 49% of the original, a decrease of 51%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h167$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$A regular hexagon has a perimeter of 42 meters. What is the area, in square meters, of the hexagon?$q$, $q$["12.25√3","73.5√3","36.75√3","147√3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Each side is 42/6 = 7. A regular hexagon is made of 6 equilateral triangles with side 7, each with area (√3/4)(7)² = 12.25√3. The total area is 6 × 12.25√3 = 73.5√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h168$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x − 4)² + (y − 2)² = 1681<br><br>The point (13, 42) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the slope of line t?$q$, $q$["−9/40","40/9","9/40","−40/9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (4, 2). The radius to (13, 42) has slope (42 − 2)/(13 − 4) = 40/9. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is −9/40.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h169$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A right circular cone and a right circular cylinder have the same base radius and the same height. The volume of the cylinder is 567π cubic inches. What is the volume, in cubic inches, of the space inside the cylinder but outside the cone?$q$, $q$["189π","567π","567/2π","378π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A cone has one-third the volume of a cylinder with the same base and height, so the cone's volume is 189π. The remaining space is 567π − 189π = 378π cubic inches.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h170$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (2x + 20)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (4x − 44)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["56","304","28","152"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 4x − 44 = ½(2x + 20). Multiplying by 2: 8x − 88 = 2x + 20, so 6x = 108 and x = 18. Then angle ABC = 4(18) − 44 = 28°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h171$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = −4x + 4 and y = −x − 5 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["48","24","27/2","32"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where −4x + 4 = −x − 5, at (3, −8), so the triangle's height is |−8| = 8. The x-intercepts are x = 1 and x = −5, so the base is 6. The area is ½(6)(8) = 24.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h172$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the bisectors of angles B and C intersect at point P inside the triangle. If the measure of angle BPC is 131°, what is the measure, in degrees, of angle A?$q$, $q$["82","49","98","41"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let angle B = 2b and angle C = 2c. In triangle BPC, angle BPC = 180° − b − c. Since 2b + 2c = 180° − A, b + c = 90° − A/2, so angle BPC = 90° + A/2. Then 131 = 90 + A/2 gives A = 82°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h173$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant IV. An equation of the circle is x² + y² − 14x + 14y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["98","42","−49","49"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (7, −7) and r² = 49 + 49 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |7| = 7. So 49 = 98 − c, giving c = 49. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h174$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 70, and BC = 24. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of CD?$q$, $q$["288/37","2450/37","840/37","47"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(70² + 24²) = 74. The area of the triangle can be computed two ways: ½(24)(70) = ½(74)(CD). So CD = (24)(70)/74 = 840/37.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h175$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the value of cos(5π/3)?$q$, $q$["−1/2","1/2","−√3/2","√3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$5π/3 radians is 300°, which lies in quadrant IV. Its reference angle is 60°, so |cos| = 1/2. In this quadrant cos is positive, so the value is 1/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h176$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar rectangular prisms have surface areas in the ratio 16 : 36. The volume of the smaller one is 256 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["864","576","384","832"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 16 : 36 means k = 6/4. So the larger volume is 256·(6/4)³ = 256·216/64 = 864.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h177$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$Each interior angle of a regular polygon has a measure that is 3 times the measure of each of its exterior angles. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["4","10","16","8"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$For a regular n-gon, each exterior angle is 360°/n and each interior angle is 180° − 360°/n. Setting 180 − 360/n = 3(360/n) gives 180 = 4(360/n), so n = 4(360)/180 = 8.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h178$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle B is 45° and the measure of angle C is 30°. The altitude from A to side BC has length 7. What is the length of side BC?$q$, $q$["14√3","7 + 7√2","7 + 7√3","14 + 7√3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let D be the foot of the altitude. Triangle ABD is a 45°-45°-90° triangle, so BD = AD = 7. Triangle ADC is a 30°-60°-90° triangle with the side opposite 30° equal to AD = 7, so DC = 7√3. Therefore BC = BD + DC = 7 + 7√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h179$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (1, −3) and (41, k) is 58. If k > −3, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−45","39","55","42"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (41 − 1)² + (k − (−3))² = 58², so 1600 + (k − (−3))² = 3364. Then (k − (−3))² = 1764, so k − (−3) = ±42. Since k > −3, k = −3 + 42 = 39.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h180$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A 39-foot ladder leans against a vertical wall. The top of the ladder touches the wall 15 feet above the level ground. Let θ be the angle between the ladder and the ground. What is the value of cos(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["5/13","12/13","5/12","13/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(39² − 15²) = 36. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (15) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (36). So cos(90° − θ) = 15/39 = 5/13, which equals sin(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h181$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 16x + y² + 14y + 32 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the length of the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["4√2","81","√113","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² + 16x + 64) + (y² + 14y + 49) = −32 + 64 + 49, so (x + 8)² + (y + 7)² = 81. The radius is √81 = 9.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h182$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with radius 15 inches, a chord has length 24 inches. What is the distance, in inches, from the center of the circle to the chord?$q$, $q$["3","13","9","12"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center bisects the chord, so it forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 15 and leg 24/2 = 12. The distance is √(15² − 12²) = √81 = 9.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h183$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$In a circle with center O, central angle AOB has a measure of 3π/5 radians. The length of arc AB is 21π units. What is the area, in square units, of sector AOB?$q$, $q$["735π","735π/2","1225π","735π/4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Arc length = rθ, so 21π = r(3π/5), giving r = 35. Sector area = ½r²θ = ½(35)²(3π/5) = 735π/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h184$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, point D lies on side AB and point E lies on side AC such that segment DE is parallel to side BC. If AD = 4, DB = 6, and DE = 40, what is the length of BC?$q$, $q$["100","60","80/3","46"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC with ratio AD/AB = 4/10. So DE/BC = 4/10, and BC = 40·10/4 = 100. A common error is to use AD/DB instead of AD/AB.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h185$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 24/7. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 2 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of sin(T)?$q$, $q$["24/25","14/25","25/7","7/25"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so sin(T) = sin(Q), and the scale factor 2 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 24/7 means the side opposite P is 24k and the side adjacent to P is 7k, with hypotenuse 25k (since 24² + 7² = 25²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 7k and the side adjacent to Q is 24k. Therefore sin(Q) = 7/25.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h186$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a right circular cylinder (including the radius and the height) is increased by 20%. By what percent does the surface area of the right circular cylinder increase?$q$, $q$["40%","20%","44%","72.8%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 1.2, area is multiplied by the square of that factor: 1.2<sup>2</sup> = 1.44. So the new surface area is 144% of the original, an increase of 44%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h187$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$An equilateral triangle has a height of 5√3 centimeters. What is the area, in square centimeters, of the triangle?$q$, $q$["50√3","25√3","25","75"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The height of an equilateral triangle with side s is (s√3)/2. So (s√3)/2 = 5√3, giving s = 10. The area is ½·base·height = ½(10)(5√3) = 25√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h188$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x − 4)² + (y − 3)² = 1369<br><br>The point (16, 38) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the slope of line t?$q$, $q$["−12/35","35/12","12/35","−35/12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (4, 3). The radius to (16, 38) has slope (38 − 3)/(16 − 4) = 35/12. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is −12/35.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h189$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A sphere is placed inside a right circular cylinder so that the sphere touches the top, the bottom, and the curved side of the cylinder. The cylinder has a volume of 686π cubic centimeters. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the sphere?$q$, $q$["686/3π","2744/3π","343π","1372/3π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If the sphere has radius R, the cylinder has radius R and height 2R, so its volume is πR²(2R) = 2πR³ = 686π. Then R³ = 343 and R = 7. The sphere's volume is (4/3)πR³ = (4/3)π(343) = 1372/3π. (In general, the sphere fills 2/3 of the cylinder.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h190$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (3x − 19)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (4x − 67)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["50","310","25","155"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 4x − 67 = ½(3x − 19). Multiplying by 2: 8x − 134 = 3x − 19, so 5x = 115 and x = 23. Then angle ABC = 4(23) − 67 = 25°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h191$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = −2x − 2 and y = −4x intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["4","2","1","6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where −2x − 2 = −4x, at (1, −4), so the triangle's height is |−4| = 4. The x-intercepts are x = −1 and x = 0, so the base is 1. The area is ½(1)(4) = 2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h192$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle A is 98°. The bisectors of angles B and C intersect at point P. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BPC?$q$, $q$["139","82","131","41"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angles B and C together measure 180° − 98° = 82°. Their halves add to 41°. In triangle BPC, angle BPC = 180° − 41° = 139°. (In general, angle BPC = 90° + A/2.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h193$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant III. An equation of the circle is x² + y² + 14x + 18y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["130","74","−81","81"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (−7, −9) and r² = 49 + 81 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |−7| = 7. So 49 = 130 − c, giving c = 81. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h194$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 36, and BC = 15. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of CD?$q$, $q$["75/13","432/13","180/13","51/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(36² + 15²) = 39. The area of the triangle can be computed two ways: ½(15)(36) = ½(39)(CD). So CD = (15)(36)/39 = 180/13.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h195$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the terminal side of an angle of 5π/4 radians in standard position intersects the unit circle at point P. What is the x-coordinate of P?$q$, $q$["√2/2","−√2/2","1/2","√3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$5π/4 radians is 225°, which lies in quadrant III. Its reference angle is 45°, so |cos| = √2/2. In this quadrant cos is negative, so the value is −√2/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h196$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar pyramids have surface areas in the ratio 1 : 25. The volume of the smaller one is 5 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["625","125","25","130"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 1 : 25 means k = 5/1. So the larger volume is 5·(5/1)³ = 5·125/1 = 625.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h197$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$Each interior angle of a regular polygon has a measure that is 3/2 times the measure of each of its exterior angles. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["5/2","7","4","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$For a regular n-gon, each exterior angle is 360°/n and each interior angle is 180° − 360°/n. Setting 180 − 360/n = 3/2(360/n) gives 180 = 5/2(360/n), so n = 5/2(360)/180 = 5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h198$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle B is 45° and the measure of angle C is 30°. The altitude from A to side BC has length 8. What is the length of side BC?$q$, $q$["16√3","8 + 8√2","8 + 8√3","16 + 8√3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let D be the foot of the altitude. Triangle ABD is a 45°-45°-90° triangle, so BD = AD = 8. Triangle ADC is a 30°-60°-90° triangle with the side opposite 30° equal to AD = 8, so DC = 8√3. Therefore BC = BD + DC = 8 + 8√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h199$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (−8, 4) and (−16, k) is 17. If k > 4, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−11","19","21","15"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (−16 − (−8))² + (k − 4)² = 17², so 64 + (k − 4)² = 289. Then (k − 4)² = 225, so k − 4 = ±15. Since k > 4, k = 4 + 15 = 19.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h200$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$A taut 20-meter support wire runs from the top of a 12-meter vertical pole to a point on the level ground. Let θ be the angle between the wire and the ground. What is the value of cos(90° − θ)?$q$, $q$["3/5","4/5","3/4","5/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The horizontal leg is √(20² − 12²) = 16. The angle 90° − θ is the other acute angle of the right triangle, at the top. For that angle, the adjacent side is the vertical leg (12) and the opposite side is the horizontal leg (16). So cos(90° − θ) = 12/20 = 3/5, which equals sin(θ).$q$),
  ($q$geo-h201$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 12x + y² + 2y + 32 = 0<br><br>The graph of the given equation in the xy-plane is a circle. What is the length of the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["4√2","5","√37","√5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Complete the square in x and y: (x² + 12x + 36) + (y² + 2y + 1) = −32 + 36 + 1, so (x + 6)² + (y + 1)² = 5. The radius is √5 = √5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h202$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle has a radius of 75 centimeters. A chord of the circle is 21 centimeters from the center of the circle. What is the length, in centimeters, of the chord?$q$, $q$["72","54","144","108"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The perpendicular from the center to a chord bisects the chord. This forms a right triangle with hypotenuse 75 (the radius) and one leg 21 (the distance to the chord). The other leg is √(75² − 21²) = √5184 = 72, which is half the chord. The chord has length 2(72) = 144.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h203$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A sector of a circle has a central angle of π/2 radians and an area of 4π square units. What is the radius of the circle?$q$, $q$["16","4","2","8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Sector area = ½r²θ, so 4π = ½r²(π/2). Then r² = 2(4π)/(π/2) = 16, so r = 4.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h204$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, point D lies on side AB and point E lies on side AC such that segment DE is parallel to side BC. If AD = 12, DB = 11, and DE = 108, what is the length of BC?$q$, $q$["207","99","1296/11","119"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since DE ∥ BC, triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC with ratio AD/AB = 12/23. So DE/BC = 12/23, and BC = 108·23/12 = 207. A common error is to use AD/DB instead of AD/AB.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h205$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle PQR, angle R is the right angle and tan(P) = 21/20. Triangle STU is similar to triangle PQR, where S, T, and U correspond to P, Q, and R, respectively, and each side of triangle STU is 3 times the length of the corresponding side of triangle PQR. What is the value of cos(T)?$q$, $q$["20/29","63/29","29/21","21/29"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar triangles have equal corresponding angles, so cos(T) = cos(Q), and the scale factor 3 does not matter. In triangle PQR, tan(P) = 21/20 means the side opposite P is 21k and the side adjacent to P is 20k, with hypotenuse 29k (since 21² + 20² = 29²). Angle Q is the other acute angle, so the side opposite Q is 20k and the side adjacent to Q is 21k. Therefore cos(Q) = 21/29.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h206$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Every linear dimension of a sphere is increased by 10%. By what percent does the volume of the sphere increase?$q$, $q$["30%","10%","33.1%","21%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When every linear dimension is multiplied by 1.1, volume is multiplied by the cube of that factor: 1.1<sup>3</sup> = 1.331. So the new volume is 133.1% of the original, an increase of 33.1%.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h207$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$A regular hexagon has a perimeter of 36 meters. What is the area, in square meters, of the hexagon?$q$, $q$["9√3","54√3","27√3","108√3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Each side is 36/6 = 6. A regular hexagon is made of 6 equilateral triangles with side 6, each with area (√3/4)(6)² = 9√3. The total area is 6 × 9√3 = 54√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h208$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$(x + 6)² + (y − 4)² = 3721<br><br>The point (5, 64) lies on the circle with the given equation in the xy-plane. Line t is tangent to the circle at this point. What is the slope of line t?$q$, $q$["−11/60","60/11","11/60","−60/11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The center is (−6, 4). The radius to (5, 64) has slope (64 − 4)/(5 − (−6)) = 60/11. A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency, so its slope is −11/60.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h209$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$A sphere is placed inside a right circular cylinder so that the sphere touches the top, the bottom, and the curved side of the cylinder. The cylinder has a volume of 250π cubic centimeters. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the sphere?$q$, $q$["250/3π","1000/3π","125π","500/3π"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If the sphere has radius R, the cylinder has radius R and height 2R, so its volume is πR²(2R) = 2πR³ = 250π. Then R³ = 125 and R = 5. The sphere's volume is (4/3)πR³ = (4/3)π(125) = 500/3π. (In general, the sphere fills 2/3 of the cylinder.)$q$),
  ($q$geo-h210$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$Points A, B, and C lie on a circle. The measure of arc AC (not containing B) is (5x + 18)°, and the measure of inscribed angle ABC is (4x − 9)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle ABC?$q$, $q$["78","282","39","141"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc: 4x − 9 = ½(5x + 18). Multiplying by 2: 8x − 18 = 5x + 18, so 3x = 36 and x = 12. Then angle ABC = 4(12) − 9 = 39°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h211$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the lines y = −4x − 26 and y = −2x − 14 intersect each other and each intersect the x-axis. What is the area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis?$q$, $q$["1","1/2","36","5/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The lines meet where −4x − 26 = −2x − 14, at (−6, −2), so the triangle's height is |−2| = 2. The x-intercepts are x = −13/2 and x = −7, so the base is 1/2. The area is ½(1/2)(2) = 1/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h212$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 3, null, $q$In isosceles triangle ABC, AB = AC. Side BC is extended past C to point D, and the measure of angle ACD is 111°. What is the measure, in degrees, of angle BAC?$q$, $q$["42","69","52","111"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Angle ACB is supplementary to angle ACD, so angle ACB = 180° − 111° = 69°. Since AB = AC, the base angles are equal: angle ABC = 69°. Then angle BAC = 180° − 2(69°) = 42°.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h213$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, null, $q$A circle in the xy-plane is tangent to the y-axis and has its center in quadrant III. An equation of the circle is x² + y² + 10x + 4y + c = 0, where c is a constant. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["29","−1","−4","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, the center is (−5, −2) and r² = 25 + 4 − c. Tangent to the y-axis means the radius equals the distance from the center to the y-axis, |−5| = 5. So 25 = 29 − c, giving c = 4. In general c = k² here, because r² = h².$q$),
  ($q$geo-h214$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In right triangle ABC, angle C is the right angle, AC = 4, and BC = 3. Point D lies on hypotenuse AB such that CD is perpendicular to AB. What is the length of CD?$q$, $q$["9/5","16/5","12/5","7/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The hypotenuse is √(4² + 3²) = 5. The area of the triangle can be computed two ways: ½(3)(4) = ½(5)(CD). So CD = (3)(4)/5 = 12/5.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h215$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$What is the value of cos(7π/4)?$q$, $q$["−√2/2","√2/2","1/2","√3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$7π/4 radians is 315°, which lies in quadrant IV. Its reference angle is 45°, so |cos| = √2/2. In this quadrant cos is positive, so the value is √2/2.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h216$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two similar rectangular prisms have surface areas in the ratio 4 : 36. The volume of the smaller one is 48 cubic units. What is the volume, in cubic units, of the larger one?$q$, $q$["1296","432","144","480"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For similar solids with linear scale factor k, areas scale by k² and volumes by k³. The area ratio 4 : 36 means k = 6/2. So the larger volume is 48·(6/2)³ = 48·216/8 = 1296.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h217$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 3, null, $q$Each interior angle of a regular polygon has a measure that is 11 times the measure of each of its exterior angles. How many sides does the polygon have?$q$, $q$["12","26","48","24"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$For a regular n-gon, each exterior angle is 360°/n and each interior angle is 180° − 360°/n. Setting 180 − 360/n = 11(360/n) gives 180 = 12(360/n), so n = 12(360)/180 = 24.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h218$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, the measure of angle B is 45° and the measure of angle C is 30°. The altitude from A to side BC has length 10. What is the length of side BC?$q$, $q$["20√3","10 + 10√2","10 + 10√3","20 + 10√3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let D be the foot of the altitude. Triangle ABD is a 45°-45°-90° triangle, so BD = AD = 10. Triangle ADC is a 30°-60°-90° triangle with the side opposite 30° equal to AD = 10, so DC = 10√3. Therefore BC = BD + DC = 10 + 10√3.$q$),
  ($q$geo-h219$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In the xy-plane, the distance between the points (5, −2) and (−11, k) is 34. If k > −2, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−32","28","32","30"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$By the distance formula, (−11 − 5)² + (k − (−2))² = 34², so 256 + (k − (−2))² = 1156. Then (k − (−2))² = 900, so k − (−2) = ±30. Since k > −2, k = −2 + 30 = 28.$q$)
